Docker Swarm

Docker Swarm is Docker's built-in container orchestration tool.
It allows you to run containers across multiple Docker machines (nodes) and manage them as one cluster.

Simple idea
Without Swarm:
Docker Host 1
 ├── Container
 ├── Container
 └── Container

With Swarm:
              Swarm Cluster
                   │
        ┌──────────┼──────────┐
        ↓          ↓          ↓
     Manager     Worker     Worker
        │          │          │
   Containers  Containers  Containers
Important terms

1. Manager node
The manager controls the Swarm:
Creates services
Decides where containers run
Maintains the desired number of replicas
Handles cluster management

2. Worker node
Workers actually run the containers/tasks assigned by the manager.

Create a Swarm
-------------------
On the manager:  docker swarm init
Docker will give you a command similar to:

docker swarm join --token <TOKEN> <MANAGER-IP>:2377
Run that command on another Docker machine to join it as a worker.


You are referring to:
docker swarm init --advertise-addr 16.22.x.x

What is --advertise-addr?
It tells the Swarm manager: "This is the IP address that other Swarm nodes should use to reach me."

For example: docker swarm init --advertise-addr 10.0.1.10    10.0.1.10 is just an example private IP address of the Manager EC2 machine.
Here:
10.0.1.10
    ↑
Manager EC2's private IP
Then Docker gives you a join command like:
docker swarm join \
  --token SWMTKN-... \
  10.0.1.10:2377

The worker uses:
Worker EC2
    |
    | connect to
    ↓
10.0.1.10:2377
    |
    ↓
Manager EC2


Why do we specify it?
An EC2 machine can have multiple IP addresses/interfaces. Swarm needs to know which IP address to advertise to the other nodes.
For AWS EC2, you normally use the private IP that the other EC2 instances can reach.


Without --advertise-addr
docker swarm init
Docker automatically chooses an IP address from the machine.

With --advertise-addr
docker swarm init --advertise-addr 10.0.1.10
You explicitly tell Docker: "Use 10.0.1.10 as the address that other Swarm nodes should use to reach this manager."
Then workers join using that address: docker swarm join --token <TOKEN> 10.0.1.10:2377
Important: --advertise-addr does not create a network and does not make containers communicate. It only tells other Swarm nodes where to reach the manager.



Docker Swarm commonly uses:

Port	Purpose
2377/TCP	Swarm management / joining the cluster   Worker/manager joins and Swarm management
7946/TCP + UDP	Communication between Swarm nodes   
4789/UDP	Overlay network traffic   Overlay network communication between containers on different nodes

So 2377 is not your application port.
For example, your application might use:
8080 → application
80   → HTTP

while:
2377 → Docker Swarm management
On AWS, your Security Group must allow TCP 2377 between the Swarm nodes.

docker swarm init → Manager
docker swarm join ... → Worker
docker service create ... → Manager
docker service scale ... → Manager
docker service ls → normally Manager


When containers are running on different EC2 machines, Swarm uses an overlay network so those containers can communicate with each other across EC2 hosts.
Example
Suppose you have 3 EC2 machines:

              Docker Swarm
                   │
       ┌───────────┼───────────┐
       ↓           ↓           ↓
    EC2-1        EC2-2        EC2-3
   Manager       Worker       Worker
       │           │           │
   web-1        web-2        web-3

Create an overlay network from the manager:       docker network create --driver overlay mynetwork


Then create the service using that network:
docker service create \
  --name web \
  --replicas 3 \
  --network mynetwork \
  nginx

Now Swarm can place the containers on different EC2 machines:

mynetwork (overlay)
────────────────────────────────
EC2-1              EC2-2              EC2-3
web-1   ←────────→  web-2   ←────────→ web-3
             communication

How does EC2-1 communicate with EC2-2?
The Docker Swarm networking layer handles this using the overlay network.
You don't need to create a separate Docker network manually on every EC2 machine.
The network is created as a Swarm-wide network, and Docker handles the networking between nodes.
Important AWS requirement

Your EC2 instances must be able to communicate with each other. The AWS Security Group needs to allow the required Swarm traffic, especially:
TCP 2377  → Swarm management
TCP/UDP 7946 → Node communication
UDP 4789 → Overlay network traffic


So the overall picture is:
             Swarm Manager
                   │
          Overlay Network
                   │
       ┌───────────┼───────────┐
       ↓           ↓           ↓
     EC2-1       EC2-2       EC2-3
    Container   Container   Container
       ↕           ↕           ↕
       └──── communication ────┘



without network menas how they communicate with other ec2
Exactly — without an overlay network, the containers cannot communicate with each other using Docker's normal container-networking mechanism across different EC2 machines.
But there are two levels of networking to understand:

1. EC2 machines can communicate
AWS networking already connects the EC2 instances:
EC2-1  ←──── AWS VPC network ────→  EC2-2
For example, EC2-1 can reach EC2-2's private IP if the Security Group allows it.

2. Containers on different EC2 machines
Suppose:
EC2-1                         EC2-2
  │                             │
web-1                         web-2

Without an overlay network, web-1 doesn't automatically get a Swarm-wide Docker network path to web-2.
With an overlay network:
EC2-1                         EC2-2
  │                             │
web-1  ←── overlay network ──→ web-2
Docker Swarm uses the EC2/VPC network underneath to transport the overlay-network traffic.




EC2 level
If both EC2 instances are in the same VPC/subnet and their Security Groups allow traffic:
EC2-1 private IP  ←────────→  EC2-2 private IP
        AWS VPC network

So EC2-1 can communicate with EC2-2.
But containers are separate
Suppose:
EC2-1                         EC2-2
┌─────────────┐              ┌─────────────┐
│  Container A│              │  Container B│
│ 172.17.0.2  │              │ 172.17.0.2  │
└─────────────┘              └─────────────┘

Notice something important: Docker's bridge network is local to each EC2.

EC2-1                         EC2-2
Docker bridge                 Docker bridge
    │                             │
Container A                  Container B

The two Docker bridge networks are not one common network.
Even though the EC2 machines can communicate, Docker doesn't automatically tell Container A:
"Send this container traffic through EC2-1's network to Container B on EC2-2."
That's why Swarm creates an overlay network:

EC2-1                         EC2-2
Container A ←── Overlay ──→ Container B
                  │
                AWS VPC



If your Manager EC2 and Worker EC2 are in the same AWS VPC, use the Manager's private IP for --advertise-addr, not its public IP.

Example:
Manager EC2
Private IP: 10.0.1.10
Public IP:   54.x.x.x


Worker EC2
Private IP: 10.0.1.20

Use:
docker swarm init --advertise-addr 10.0.1.10

Then the Worker connects to:
docker swarm join --token <TOKEN> 10.0.1.10:2377

Why?
Worker EC2
    │
    │ AWS VPC private network
    ↓
Manager EC2
10.0.1.10:2377

The private IP is used for communication between EC2 instances inside the VPC.
So remember:
Same VPC → use the Manager's private IP for Swarm communication.


  
If the Manager and Worker EC2 are in different VPCs, they cannot normally communicate using their private IPs automatically.
Same VPC
VPC
 ├── Manager EC2: 10.0.1.10
 └── Worker EC2:  10.0.2.10
          ↕
      Can communicate
Different VPCs
VPC-A                         VPC-B
Manager                       Worker
10.0.1.10                     10.0.1.20
     ✕  ← no automatic connection →  ✕

You need to establish connectivity between the VPCs, commonly using VPC Peering or Transit Gateway, plus the appropriate route tables and security-group/NACL rules.

Then the Swarm nodes can communicate.

VPC-A                    VPC-B
Manager  ←────────────→  Worker
          VPC connection

For Docker Swarm, you must also allow the required ports between the nodes:
2377/TCP
7946/TCP + UDP
4789/UDP
So the important idea is:
Different VPC ≠ impossible. It just requires network connectivity between the VPCs first.


then now the conatiner can communicate overklay network or vpc peering
Both work together, but they have different jobs.

Think of 2 layers
Container A
    ↓
Docker Overlay Network
    ↓
EC2-1
    ↓
AWS VPC / VPC Peering
    ↓
EC2-2
    ↓
Docker Overlay Network
    ↓
Container B
What does each one do?

VPC Peering provides the underlying connection between the EC2 machines:

VPC-A                         VPC-B
Manager EC2  ← VPC Peering →  Worker EC2

Overlay network provides the Docker container-to-container network across those EC2 machines:

Container A  ←── Overlay ──→  Container B

So if your Manager and Worker are in different VPCs:

Configure VPC Peering between the VPCs.
Add the appropriate routes in both VPC route tables.
Allow Swarm ports in Security Groups.
Create the Docker overlay network.
Swarm uses the VPC connection underneath to carry the overlay traffic.
Full picture
        VPC-A                         VPC-B
┌──────────────────┐          ┌──────────────────┐
│ Manager EC2      │          │ Worker EC2       │
│                  │          │                  │
│ Container A      │          │ Container B      │
└───────┬──────────┘          └──────────┬───────┘
        │                                │
        └──── Docker Overlay Network ────┘
                       │
                  VPC Peering
                       │
                 AWS network

So: VPC Peering connects the EC2 machines; Overlay Network connects the containers. The overlay network depends on the underlying EC2 network being reachable.




Create a service
For example:
docker service create --name web --replicas 3 -p 80:80 nginx
every service going to have unique ID
  
This means:
Service: web
Replicas: 3
Image: nginx
Port: 80

Swarm tries to keep 3 nginx containers running across the cluster.

docker service ls
docker service ps web    detailed information


Scale
If you want 5 containers:
docker service scale web=5
Swarm creates additional replicas.







  
